Subject: [PATCH 07/21] core: Fire Emblem Fates' randomizer laid in memory
 after the mode pick

The Fates port's owner asked (its docs/DECISIONS.md F-044 and addendum,
engine build OK 2026-11-20) to set every randomizer setting inside the
game, right after picking Casual or Classic for a new game, instead of
in a separate PLAY RANDOMIZED profile that needs its own game folder and
a restart. Zakuro's mods layer is laid once at boot, so this cannot use
it. New module zakuro-core fates_rando (not wired into a frame yet;
nothing calls it, so every run is unchanged):

- Patch::parse reads the patch the port's Python randomizer writes
  (tools/randomizer/rando.py make --patch-out): per file (GameData.bin.lz
  or a chapter placement file GameData/Dispos/**), value cells with
  their original and new bytes, and label pointer cells with the
  original and new label text, where the new label lives (GameData's or
  the file's own string pool) and the original pointer's file value.
- gamedata_start / dispos_now find the game's copies the way the game
  does: GameData::Initialize 0x004E6954 keeps its ArchiveFile at
  *0x006D3868 and table pointers count from [[archive + 4] + 112] + 32;
  map::Dispos::Load 0x0039ED9C keeps the current placement file at
  *0x006D6DC8 (name at +0, ArchiveFile at +16). The port's robot read
  both from real memory (round 50): every byte equals the original file.
- apply_file checks every cell of a file against its original value
  (the label text, for pointers, and that each new label is where the
  patch says) and then writes all of them, or writes nothing. revert
  writes the original values back. Run keeps what is laid: GameData
  once, each placement file once per load.
- NewGameWatch: the frame of the mode pick, from GameConfigData
  (*0x006DA0F8) word +4 changing while byte +0xB stays 1 (robot: frame
  4019 of the new-game route; a save load changes the word with +0xB
  going to 0).
- slot_written / slot_run: a side file per save file
  (<data>/rando/0004000000179800/slots/Chapter0.json ...) naming the run
  a randomized save belongs to.

Our own code and words; no game data. Unit tests use a fake memory.
